1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,079
Look at the fuel gauge in your

2
00:00:02,079 --> 00:00:02,356
car.

3
00:00:02,536 --> 00:00:05,138
Next to the little fuel pump symbol,

4
00:00:05,138 --> 00:00:06,728
there is a tiny arrow.

5
00:00:06,908 --> 00:00:09,523
Most drivers have never noticed it,

6
00:00:09,523 --> 00:00:12,363
and it answers a question every driver

7
00:00:12,363 --> 00:00:14,605
has asked at a petrol station.

8
00:00:14,785 --> 00:00:17,237
Your car is full of small parts

9
00:00:17,237 --> 00:00:19,847
like that, hiding in plain sight.

10
00:00:20,027 --> 00:00:21,951
Today, ten of them,

11
00:00:21,951 --> 00:00:24,381
and why each one exists.

12
00:00:24,731 --> 00:00:26,797
These are not expensive parts.

13
00:00:26,977 --> 00:00:28,118
They are dots,

14
00:00:28,118 --> 00:00:30,646
lines, arrows and little hairs.

15
00:00:30,826 --> 00:00:33,062
But each one was added by an

16
00:00:33,062 --> 00:00:35,697
engineer to solve a real problem,

17
00:00:35,697 --> 00:00:36,814
a safety risk,

18
00:00:36,814 --> 00:00:39,130
a glue that would fail in the

19
00:00:39,130 --> 00:00:41,685
sun, or a driver standing at the

20
00:00:41,685 --> 00:00:42,564
wrong pump.

21
00:00:42,744 --> 00:00:43,842
Once you know them,

22
00:00:43,842 --> 00:00:45,634
you will see them on every car.

23
00:00:45,985 --> 00:00:49,112
As always, every fact here comes from

24
00:00:49,112 --> 00:00:50,379
a named source,

25
00:00:50,379 --> 00:00:54,943
car makers, safety agencies and automotive references,

26
00:00:54,943 --> 00:00:57,478
all listed in the description.

27
00:00:57,658 --> 00:00:59,423
Let's start with the arrow.

28
00:00:59,774 --> 00:01:01,806
Why is there a little arrow next

29
00:01:01,806 --> 00:01:03,013
to your fuel gauge?

30
00:01:03,193 --> 00:01:05,231
It tells you which side of the

31
00:01:05,231 --> 00:01:06,861
car the fuel door is on.

32
00:01:07,041 --> 00:01:08,982
If the arrow points left,

33
00:01:08,982 --> 00:01:11,000
the filler is on the left.

34
00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:12,411
If it points right,

35
00:01:12,411 --> 00:01:13,642
it is on the right.

36
00:01:13,822 --> 00:01:16,329
It exists for the moment you drive

37
00:01:16,329 --> 00:01:18,614
a rented or borrowed car into a

38
00:01:18,614 --> 00:01:21,268
petrol station and do not know which

39
00:01:21,268 --> 00:01:22,448
side to stop on.

40
00:01:22,798 --> 00:01:25,179
There is no single rule for which

41
00:01:25,179 --> 00:01:27,128
side the fuel door goes on.

42
00:01:27,308 --> 00:01:30,040
Car makers choose based on the layout

43
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:31,664
of the car underneath,

44
00:01:31,664 --> 00:01:32,772
where the tank,

45
00:01:32,772 --> 00:01:35,356
the exhaust and the other parts fit

46
00:01:35,356 --> 00:01:35,725
best.

47
00:01:35,905 --> 00:01:38,279
So two cars from the same brand

48
00:01:38,279 --> 00:01:41,188
can have the filler on opposite sides,

49
00:01:41,188 --> 00:01:44,021
and drivers guess wrong all the time.

50
00:01:44,371 --> 00:01:46,851
The arrow is often credited to Jim

51
00:01:46,851 --> 00:01:48,822
Moylan, a designer at Ford,

52
00:01:48,822 --> 00:01:50,938
who suggested it in a memo in

53
00:01:50,938 --> 00:01:53,929
the nineteen eighties after pulling up to

54
00:01:53,929 --> 00:01:56,045
the wrong side of the pump on

55
00:01:56,045 --> 00:01:56,921
a rainy day.

56
00:01:57,101 --> 00:02:00,176
It appeared on Ford cars and slowly

57
00:02:00,176 --> 00:02:03,075
spread to almost every car in the

58
00:02:03,075 --> 00:02:03,602
world.

59
00:02:03,782 --> 00:02:05,155
One small idea,

60
00:02:05,155 --> 00:02:09,273
saving millions of drivers a few embarrassing

61
00:02:09,273 --> 00:02:10,005
seconds.

62
00:02:10,356 --> 00:02:12,449
Why are there black dots around the

63
00:02:12,449 --> 00:02:13,885
edge of your windscreen?

64
00:02:14,065 --> 00:02:16,270
They are part of a black band

65
00:02:16,270 --> 00:02:17,486
called the frit,

66
00:02:17,486 --> 00:02:20,300
a ceramic paint baked into the glass.

67
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:23,076
The windscreen is glued to the car,

68
00:02:23,076 --> 00:02:25,820
and that glue is damaged by sunlight.

69
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,229
The black band blocks the sun,

70
00:02:28,229 --> 00:02:30,830
so the glue stays strong for years.

71
00:02:31,180 --> 00:02:33,445
The band also gives the glue a

72
00:02:33,445 --> 00:02:35,106
rough surface to grip,

73
00:02:35,106 --> 00:02:37,448
and it hides the glue from view

74
00:02:37,448 --> 00:02:39,260
so the edge looks clean.

75
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:41,356
The dots are there because of heat.

76
00:02:41,536 --> 00:02:44,363
A solid black band heats up much

77
00:02:44,363 --> 00:02:46,307
more than clear glass.

78
00:02:46,487 --> 00:02:49,526
Fading the black into smaller and smaller

79
00:02:49,526 --> 00:02:52,046
dots spreads the heat more gently,

80
00:02:52,046 --> 00:02:54,417
and reduces stress in the glass.

81
00:02:54,766 --> 00:02:57,169
The dots also make the change from

82
00:02:57,169 --> 00:02:59,643
black to clear look smoother to the

83
00:02:59,643 --> 00:03:01,975
eye, and they can reduce a slight

84
00:03:01,975 --> 00:03:04,519
distortion at the edge of the glass.

85
00:03:04,699 --> 00:03:06,767
You will find a patch of dots

86
00:03:06,767 --> 00:03:09,049
behind the rear view mirror too,

87
00:03:09,049 --> 00:03:11,617
shading the area where the sun would

88
00:03:11,617 --> 00:03:13,685
shine between the sun visors.

89
00:03:13,865 --> 00:03:16,648
Nothing on the windscreen is there by

90
00:03:16,648 --> 00:03:17,325
accident.

91
00:03:17,674 --> 00:03:20,572
Why are there small bumps hidden inside

92
00:03:20,572 --> 00:03:22,504
the grooves of your tyres?

93
00:03:22,684 --> 00:03:24,902
They are tread wear indicators.

94
00:03:25,082 --> 00:03:27,017
Each bump is a little bar of

95
00:03:27,017 --> 00:03:29,505
rubber that sits about one point six

96
00:03:29,505 --> 00:03:32,477
millimetres above the bottom of the groove.

97
00:03:32,657 --> 00:03:34,997
When the tyre wears down to the

98
00:03:34,997 --> 00:03:36,355
level of the bars,

99
00:03:36,355 --> 00:03:38,242
it is time to replace it.

100
00:03:38,591 --> 00:03:41,558
One point six millimetres is the legal

101
00:03:41,558 --> 00:03:44,524
minimum tread depth in many countries,

102
00:03:44,524 --> 00:03:46,788
including the European Union.

103
00:03:46,968 --> 00:03:49,526
The grooves in a tyre carry water

104
00:03:49,526 --> 00:03:52,084
away, so the rubber can touch the

105
00:03:52,084 --> 00:03:52,471
road.

106
00:03:52,651 --> 00:03:55,406
When the grooves become too shallow,

107
00:03:55,406 --> 00:03:58,007
water stays under the tyre and the

108
00:03:58,007 --> 00:04:00,226
car can start to float on it,

109
00:04:00,226 --> 00:04:02,369
which is called aquaplaning.

110
00:04:02,718 --> 00:04:04,162
You do not need a gauge to

111
00:04:04,162 --> 00:04:04,495
check.

112
00:04:04,675 --> 00:04:07,032
When the surface of the tread becomes

113
00:04:07,032 --> 00:04:08,306
level with the bars,

114
00:04:08,306 --> 00:04:09,899
the tyre is at its limit.

115
00:04:10,079 --> 00:04:12,986
Many tyres also mark the position of

116
00:04:12,986 --> 00:04:15,651
the bars with a small triangle or

117
00:04:15,651 --> 00:04:17,347
logo on the shoulder.

118
00:04:17,527 --> 00:04:19,940
A tiny bump of rubber turns a

119
00:04:19,940 --> 00:04:23,518
safety check into something anyone can see.

120
00:04:23,867 --> 00:04:26,188
Why do new tyres have little rubber

121
00:04:26,188 --> 00:04:27,977
hairs sticking out of them?

122
00:04:28,157 --> 00:04:30,208
They are a side effect of how

123
00:04:30,208 --> 00:04:31,269
tyres are made.

124
00:04:31,449 --> 00:04:33,871
A tyre is pressed into a hot

125
00:04:33,871 --> 00:04:37,159
mould, and the rubber flows into every

126
00:04:37,159 --> 00:04:37,765
corner.

127
00:04:37,945 --> 00:04:40,925
Air trapped inside the mould would leave

128
00:04:40,925 --> 00:04:42,490
holes and weak spots,

129
00:04:42,490 --> 00:04:44,726
so the mould has tiny holes to

130
00:04:44,726 --> 00:04:46,141
let the air escape.

131
00:04:46,491 --> 00:04:47,880
As the air escapes,

132
00:04:47,880 --> 00:04:50,657
a little rubber flows into those holes

133
00:04:50,657 --> 00:04:50,950
too.

134
00:04:51,130 --> 00:04:53,098
When the tyre comes out of the

135
00:04:53,098 --> 00:04:55,460
mould, the rubber in the holes stays

136
00:04:55,460 --> 00:04:56,969
attached as thin hairs.

137
00:04:57,149 --> 00:05:00,017
Tyre makers call them vent spews.

138
00:05:00,197 --> 00:05:02,124
They have no job on the road.

139
00:05:02,304 --> 00:05:04,636
They are the fingerprints of air leaving

140
00:05:04,636 --> 00:05:05,219
the mould.

141
00:05:05,568 --> 00:05:06,719
They are harmless,

142
00:05:06,719 --> 00:05:08,893
and they wear away after the first

143
00:05:08,893 --> 00:05:10,363
few hundred kilometres.

144
00:05:10,543 --> 00:05:13,096
Some tyre makers now use a different

145
00:05:13,096 --> 00:05:15,507
kind of vent that leaves no hairs,

146
00:05:15,507 --> 00:05:17,776
to give the tyre a cleaner look.

147
00:05:17,956 --> 00:05:19,289
But if you see them,

148
00:05:19,289 --> 00:05:20,955
you know the tyre is new,

149
00:05:20,955 --> 00:05:23,087
and that it was made without air

150
00:05:23,087 --> 00:05:24,086
bubbles inside.

151
00:05:24,436 --> 00:05:26,658
Why do many rear car doors have

152
00:05:26,658 --> 00:05:29,022
a small fixed window at the back?

153
00:05:29,202 --> 00:05:30,770
Because of the wheel.

154
00:05:30,950 --> 00:05:33,134
The rear wheel arch cuts into the

155
00:05:33,134 --> 00:05:34,723
bottom of the rear door.

156
00:05:34,903 --> 00:05:37,761
When the main window slides down into

157
00:05:37,761 --> 00:05:40,232
the door, it would hit the wheel

158
00:05:40,232 --> 00:05:42,240
arch before it disappears.

159
00:05:42,420 --> 00:05:44,691
So the window is made smaller,

160
00:05:44,691 --> 00:05:46,810
and the gap is filled with a

161
00:05:46,810 --> 00:05:48,400
fixed piece of glass.

162
00:05:48,749 --> 00:05:49,810
That is why,

163
00:05:49,810 --> 00:05:50,960
in many cars,

164
00:05:50,960 --> 00:05:54,407
the rear window only goes down partway,

165
00:05:54,407 --> 00:05:57,237
or the door has a small triangle

166
00:05:57,237 --> 00:05:59,535
of glass that never moves.

167
00:05:59,715 --> 00:06:02,646
Engineers would rather have a fully opening

168
00:06:02,646 --> 00:06:04,895
window, but the shape of the door

169
00:06:04,895 --> 00:06:07,621
simply does not leave enough room inside

170
00:06:07,621 --> 00:06:09,189
it for the whole glass.

171
00:06:09,538 --> 00:06:12,395
The small fixed glass also lets light

172
00:06:12,395 --> 00:06:15,329
in and gives passengers a better view,

173
00:06:15,329 --> 00:06:17,568
so the back of the cabin does

174
00:06:17,568 --> 00:06:18,803
not feel closed.

175
00:06:18,983 --> 00:06:21,528
Some modern cars hide it behind a

176
00:06:21,528 --> 00:06:23,070
black panel instead.

177
00:06:23,250 --> 00:06:25,598
The next time a rear window stops

178
00:06:25,598 --> 00:06:27,945
halfway, you will know there is a

179
00:06:27,945 --> 00:06:29,368
wheel just below it.

180
00:06:29,718 --> 00:06:31,950
Why do cars have a third brake

181
00:06:31,950 --> 00:06:34,107
light, high up in the middle?

182
00:06:34,287 --> 00:06:36,213
So the driver behind can see you

183
00:06:36,213 --> 00:06:38,380
brake, even through the car in front

184
00:06:38,380 --> 00:06:38,861
of them.

185
00:06:39,041 --> 00:06:41,700
The two normal brake lights are low,

186
00:06:41,700 --> 00:06:44,062
at the height of the other car's

187
00:06:44,062 --> 00:06:44,579
bumper.

188
00:06:44,759 --> 00:06:46,962
The third one is at eye level,

189
00:06:46,962 --> 00:06:49,900
often visible through the windows of the

190
00:06:49,900 --> 00:06:51,075
cars in between.

191
00:06:51,425 --> 00:06:52,994
In the United States,

192
00:06:52,994 --> 00:06:55,459
it became required on new cars in

193
00:06:55,459 --> 00:06:57,103
the nineteen eighties,

194
00:06:57,103 --> 00:07:00,165
after studies found it helped reduce rear

195
00:07:00,165 --> 00:07:01,062
end crashes.

196
00:07:01,242 --> 00:07:03,250
Many other countries followed.

197
00:07:03,430 --> 00:07:04,945
It has a special job,

198
00:07:04,945 --> 00:07:07,398
because it lights up only when you

199
00:07:07,398 --> 00:07:07,830
brake.

200
00:07:08,010 --> 00:07:10,371
It is never confused with the tail

201
00:07:10,371 --> 00:07:11,482
lights at night.

202
00:07:11,831 --> 00:07:14,441
Today it is often a thin strip

203
00:07:14,441 --> 00:07:16,964
of LEDs in the rear window or

204
00:07:16,964 --> 00:07:18,008
the spoiler.

205
00:07:18,188 --> 00:07:20,495
LEDs light up a fraction of a

206
00:07:20,495 --> 00:07:22,802
second faster than old bulbs,

207
00:07:22,802 --> 00:07:25,825
which gives the driver behind a little

208
00:07:25,825 --> 00:07:27,337
more time to react.

209
00:07:27,517 --> 00:07:28,891
At highway speed,

210
00:07:28,891 --> 00:07:31,478
that fraction of a second can be

211
00:07:31,478 --> 00:07:34,227
a few metres of stopping distance.

212
00:07:34,577 --> 00:07:37,144
Why do modern cars have a little

213
00:07:37,144 --> 00:07:38,908
shark fin on the roof?

214
00:07:39,088 --> 00:07:41,108
It is an antenna cover.

215
00:07:41,288 --> 00:07:43,465
Cars used to have a long metal

216
00:07:43,465 --> 00:07:44,771
rod for the radio.

217
00:07:44,951 --> 00:07:48,071
Today, a car may need antennas for

218
00:07:48,071 --> 00:07:51,007
the radio, satellite navigation,

219
00:07:51,007 --> 00:07:54,402
phone connection and emergency calls.

220
00:07:54,582 --> 00:07:57,058
The fin holds several of them in

221
00:07:57,058 --> 00:07:58,761
one small, hard shell.

222
00:07:59,111 --> 00:08:01,248
The roof is the best place for

223
00:08:01,248 --> 00:08:04,239
antennas, because it has the clearest view

224
00:08:04,239 --> 00:08:05,022
of the sky,

225
00:08:05,022 --> 00:08:07,301
and the metal roof below acts as

226
00:08:07,301 --> 00:08:09,723
a reflector that helps the signal.

227
00:08:09,903 --> 00:08:13,096
A long rod antenna makes wind noise,

228
00:08:13,096 --> 00:08:14,781
adds a little drag,

229
00:08:14,781 --> 00:08:17,087
and can be broken in a car

230
00:08:17,087 --> 00:08:17,531
wash.

231
00:08:17,711 --> 00:08:19,998
The fin solves all three.

232
00:08:20,347 --> 00:08:22,484
The shape is not just for style.

233
00:08:22,664 --> 00:08:25,469
A low, curved fin lets air flow

234
00:08:25,469 --> 00:08:27,007
smoothly over it,

235
00:08:27,007 --> 00:08:29,270
with less noise at speed.

236
00:08:29,450 --> 00:08:32,713
Inside, there are small circuit boards tuned

237
00:08:32,713 --> 00:08:34,419
to each kind of signal.

238
00:08:34,599 --> 00:08:37,362
The fin is a tiny communication tower

239
00:08:37,362 --> 00:08:39,603
that rides on top of your car.

240
00:08:39,952 --> 00:08:42,129
Why are there thin lines across your

241
00:08:42,129 --> 00:08:42,855
rear window?

242
00:08:43,035 --> 00:08:44,556
They are heating wires.

243
00:08:44,736 --> 00:08:47,239
The lines are made of a conductive

244
00:08:47,239 --> 00:08:50,111
paint, printed onto the glass and baked

245
00:08:50,111 --> 00:08:50,332
in.

246
00:08:50,512 --> 00:08:53,152
When you press the defrost button,

247
00:08:53,152 --> 00:08:55,559
electricity flows through them,

248
00:08:55,559 --> 00:08:57,967
and they warm up just enough to

249
00:08:57,967 --> 00:08:59,986
clear ice, frost and mist.

250
00:09:00,335 --> 00:09:03,282
The lines are spaced so heat spreads

251
00:09:03,282 --> 00:09:05,246
evenly across the glass.

252
00:09:05,426 --> 00:09:07,395
They run across the window,

253
00:09:07,395 --> 00:09:09,802
connected at each side by a wider

254
00:09:09,802 --> 00:09:11,916
strip where the wires attach.

255
00:09:12,096 --> 00:09:14,562
A broken line leaves a stripe of

256
00:09:14,562 --> 00:09:17,335
frost, which is why repair kits sell

257
00:09:17,335 --> 00:09:20,185
a small bottle of conductive paint to

258
00:09:20,185 --> 00:09:21,187
reconnect it.

259
00:09:21,537 --> 00:09:22,637
On some cars,

260
00:09:22,637 --> 00:09:24,752
a few of the lines have a

261
00:09:24,752 --> 00:09:25,682
second job.

262
00:09:25,862 --> 00:09:27,816
They are the radio antenna,

263
00:09:27,816 --> 00:09:29,264
hidden in the glass.

264
00:09:29,444 --> 00:09:31,749
That is why some cars have lines

265
00:09:31,749 --> 00:09:33,839
that do not reach all the way

266
00:09:33,839 --> 00:09:34,343
across.

267
00:09:34,523 --> 00:09:35,802
A few thin lines,

268
00:09:35,802 --> 00:09:39,187
keeping your view clear and sometimes playing

269
00:09:39,187 --> 00:09:40,015
your music.

270
00:09:40,364 --> 00:09:42,488
Why do the low beams of your

271
00:09:42,488 --> 00:09:43,929
car shine unevenly?

272
00:09:44,109 --> 00:09:45,966
Look at the light on a wall,

273
00:09:45,966 --> 00:09:47,889
and you will see the top edge

274
00:09:47,889 --> 00:09:48,950
is not straight.

275
00:09:49,130 --> 00:09:51,070
On one side it is lower,

276
00:09:51,070 --> 00:09:53,577
and on the other side it rises.

277
00:09:53,757 --> 00:09:55,742
It is designed that way.

278
00:09:55,922 --> 00:09:58,943
The low side faces oncoming traffic,

279
00:09:58,943 --> 00:10:01,797
so you do not blind other drivers.

280
00:10:02,146 --> 00:10:04,802
The higher side lights the edge of

281
00:10:04,802 --> 00:10:07,692
the road, where people walk and signs

282
00:10:07,692 --> 00:10:08,160
stand.

283
00:10:08,340 --> 00:10:10,986
That way, you see further along the

284
00:10:10,986 --> 00:10:13,784
side of the road without shining into

285
00:10:13,784 --> 00:10:14,842
anyone's eyes.

286
